## Standing Rules

<!-- IF PRIMARY_GOAL=fat_loss -->
### Fat-loss rules
- **Protein floor:** target ≥1.8 g/kg of current bodyweight per day. Hold this even as bodyweight drops.
- **Weight trend (7-day rolling average):**
  - On track (0.5–0.8 kg/wk loss): hold calories and volume.
  - Slow (<0.4 kg/wk for 10+ days): −150 kcal/day or add one cardio session.
  - Fast (>1.0 kg/wk for 10+ days): +150 kcal/day (faster loss = more muscle loss).
- **Lift progression (cut-mode):** load stays week-to-week unless a working set feels ≤ RPE 6 — only then add 2.5 kg or 1 rep. If a top set hits RPE 9 unprompted, drop load 5% next session.
- **Cardio/conditioning progression:** increase volume by one unit (e.g., +5 laps, +5 min) in one session per week if recovery is fine.
- Prioritize muscle preservation over calorie deficit aggression. Do not chase the scale faster than the targets above.
<!-- ENDIF -->

<!-- IF PRIMARY_GOAL=muscle_gain -->
### Muscle-gain rules
- **Calorie surplus:** maintain a modest surplus per user-provided target. If weekly weight gain exceeds 0.5 kg, flag for calorie review.
- **Progressive overload:** add load (2.5 kg) or reps when the top working set is ≤ RPE 7 for two consecutive sessions on that exercise.
- **Volume:** prioritize compound lifts; accessory volume follows. Weekly set count per muscle group: increase by 1–2 sets every 3–4 weeks as tolerance allows.
- **Cardio:** keep conditioning minimal (1–2 short sessions) to support recovery and cardiovascular health without eating into surplus.
<!-- ENDIF -->

<!-- IF PRIMARY_GOAL=strength -->
### Strength rules
- **Rep schemes:** primary lifts at 3–5 reps, 3–5 sets. Accessory work at 6–8 reps.
- **Progressive overload:** add load (2.5 kg) when the main lift top set is ≤ RPE 7. Stall for two sessions → deload 10% and rebuild.
- **Deload:** planned deload every 4–6 weeks (reduce volume 40–50%, hold intensity).
- **Nutrition:** calorie-neutral to slight surplus. Protein ≥1.8 g/kg. No aggressive cut during a strength block.
- **Cardio:** minimal — 1 short session for general health only. Do not programme conditioning that competes with recovery from heavy compound work.
<!-- ENDIF -->

<!-- IF PRIMARY_GOAL=maintenance -->
### Maintenance rules
- **Stability target:** hold bodyweight ±1 kg of current weight. Flag any trend beyond that for review.
- **Load:** hold all loads and volumes unless deviation signals warrant change. No progressive overload pressure.
- **Nutrition:** calorie-neutral; protein at minimum 1.6 g/kg.
- **Cardio:** hold current volume. Increase only if user requests it.
<!-- ENDIF -->

<!-- IF PRIMARY_GOAL=general_fitness -->
### General fitness rules
- **Variety bias:** rotate stimuli every 3–4 weeks to prevent accommodation and sustain motivation.
- **Progression:** gradual, across all modalities. No single-metric obsession.
- **Balance:** roughly equal emphasis on strength, cardiovascular fitness, and mobility/flexibility based on user preference.
- **Load:** increase when RPE ≤6 consistently across two sessions. Back off if RPE >8 on multi-session patterns.
<!-- ENDIF -->

<!-- IF PRIMARY_GOAL=sport_specific -->
### Sport-specific rules
- **Sport schedule takes priority.** Programme gym and conditioning work around practice/competition calendar, not vice versa.
- **Pre-competition:** reduce training load 1–2 weeks out (taper). Flag any high-intensity gym session inside the taper window for user sign-off.
- **In-season:** maintain, do not build. Reduce volume aggressively if sport load is high.
- **Off-season:** build base; progressive overload applies. Align periodisation with the user's competitive calendar.
- Provide general fatigue and recovery guidance; defer sport-specific technical coaching to the user's sport coach.
<!-- ENDIF -->

